A||||4||88fierodrvr||belt tensioner||||1||
Z||000000||88fierodrvr||09-23-2001||01:14 PM||||Tired of the squeal that I get every time I start the engine or rev it up, I decided to pull the belt and check all of my pulleys for noise. The only problem was that the tensioner was released, when i attempt to release tension, it tightens it. So I am waiting for the correct version of the tensioner for an 88 2.5 from a Fiero to install, (it appears that front engine cars using this motor release in the opposite direction).<P>Just wanted to share my frustration, and finding with others.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-23-2001||02:34 PM||||That I'm aware of, the FWD tensioner will not work on Fiero. It sounds like the one you have is just plain shot. common problem.<P>While the belts is off clean all the pulleys with brake parts cleaner, Simple Green or CitriSolv and water.<P>When you replace the tenioner replace the belt. Even if this one looks ok it probalby not. Save the old one for an emergency spare.<P>there's a bunch of notes in my cave on this.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||09-23-2001||03:37 PM||||Finally got around to replacing my tensioner. $48 at Lou Sobh Pontiac in West Atlanta. <P>My question is this... The old tensioner had a silicone gasket. Not just sealer. It really <I>looks</I> like a gasket. <BR>The new tensioner has a cork gasket. Do I need to use any kind of sealer? <BR>BTW... This turned out to be lots easier than I expected. Once I removed the D@!* sound insulation from the side wall.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||09-24-2001||12:40 AM||||Ogre's right...btw welcome to the forum...glad to see another Tennessean here!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-24-2001||08:47 PM||||Sealer probably isn't needed. On cork I give them a thin film of Permatex Super 300 or Balkamp Aviation sealer. If you use RTV just make a thin film on the cork. very thin. All you want is to seal any poors or scratches.<P>Main problem with sealers is the vast majority of people use way too much of the crap.||||reg||1
